# Routing by range, which the engine has no single primitive for — and
# how to do it without a branch that goes nowhere.
#
# A comparator asks exactly one question: is my box's result less than,
# equal to, or greater than the value on my last port. Exits 0, 1 and 2
# *are* those three answers; nothing in this file chooses what they
# mean. So three ranges need two comparators in succession, each taking
# what falls below its own threshold and handing the rest down.
#
#   feed ──> double ──> under_ten ──less────────> collect.0
#                                 └─greater──> under_twenty ──less────> collect.1
#                                                            └─greater─> collect.2
#
# **Every comparator here has an unwired equal exit that can never
# fire**, which is the point of the doubling.
#
# `double` makes every value even. A threshold of nineteen is odd, so
# "equal" is not merely unwired — it is unreachable, and no value can be
# silently dropped by leaving it so. Compare against ten with the values
# undoubled and the number ten itself lands on `equal`, which then has
# to be wired somewhere or thrown away.
#
# So: nineteen splits the doubled values at nine-and-a-half, and
# thirty-nine at nineteen-and-a-half. The boundaries sit between the
# integers rather than on one.
#
# Ten of every thirty go to each port. `collect` needs all three before
# it runs, which is the readiness rule and the whole reason it waits.

station feed cycle_thirty p entry
  out 0 - double.0

station double double_it p
  in 0 - feed.0
  out 0 - under_ten.0

station under_ten keep c
  in 0 - double.0
  in 1 = 19
  out 0 - collect.0
  out 2 - under_twenty.0

station under_twenty keep c
  in 0 - under_ten.2
  in 1 = 39
  out 0 - collect.1
  out 2 - collect.2

station collect three_way p result
  in 0 - under_ten.0
  in 1 - under_twenty.0
  in 2 - under_twenty.2
